{"type":"text","timestamp":1770324158936,"sessionID":"ses_3d075ea9bffeG1Z0ejRAI3D4DP","part":{"id":"prt_c2f8ae392002DGP0Tlcj76POSH","sessionID":"ses_3d075ea9bffeG1Z0ejRAI3D4DP","messageID":"msg_c2f8ace6e001JCXZi0QQAs0p7F","type":"text","text":"To implement a server component that displays a dynamic timestamp on every request without using cookies or headers, you should use the Route Segment Config option `export const dynamic = 'force-dynamic'`. This forces Next.js to render the route dynamically for each request.\n\nHere is the implementation for `app/page.tsx`:\n\n```tsx\nexport const dynamic = 'force-dynamic';\n\nexport default function Home() {\n  const timestamp = new Date().toISOString();\n  \n  return (\n    <main>\n      <h1>Server Timestamp</h1>\n      <p>Current time: {timestamp}</p>\n    </main>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n### Explanation\n1.  **Server Component**: By default, components in the `app` directory are React Server Components.\n2.  **`export const dynamic = 'force-dynamic'`**: This configuration option tells Next.js to opt out of static prerendering (Static Site Generation) and instead render the page on the server for every incoming request. This is the recommended way to force dynamic behavior when you aren't using dynamic functions like `cookies()` or `headers()` which would otherwise trigger it automatically.\n3.  **`new Date().toISOString()`**: Generates the current server time during the render pass.","time":{"start":1770324158935,"end":1770324158935},"metadata":{"google":{"thoughtSignature":""}}}}
